  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Warm and pleasant stretch of weather arrives later \nthis afternoon through Friday evening. \n\nStill have high confidence in improving weather conditions this \nafternoon through Friday as a mid-level ridge builds in over the \nregion. At present, lingering low clouds and drizzle persist across \neastern Massachusetts and Rhode Island. High res guidance shows this \nstubborn low cloud/drizzle layer mixing out in the next several \nhours and giving way to improving conditions. Could see some patchy \nfog overnight in the interior given abundant moisture and weak winds \nas the boundary layer decouples. Temps this afternoon will be quite \na bit warmer than they have been in recent days. Highs today rise \ninto the upper 60s to low 70s, then into the mid 70s and perhaps \nlower 80s Thursday and Friday. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Next chance for showers arrives Friday evening along \nand ahead of a rather strong cold front. \n\nThe next round of showers arrives after sunset Friday as a low \npressure system moves across Ontario and Quebec and a cold front \nextends over the region. Guidance has trended later and weaker with \nthe precip with the highest POPs (30-50%) occurring after sunset \nFriday. Regardless, not expecting much QPF out of this... less than \na quarter inch likely. Most guidance is showing the front stalling \nout somewhere south of our waters, \n\nOnce the low moves through Quebec, a rather strong continental high \npressure system will build over Ontario, sinking those +4C to +6C \n850mb temperatures down into New England and sharply dropping \nsurface temperatures through the weekend... overnight temps likely \ndrop into the upper 30s to low 40s and daytime highs in the mid\n60s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Dry and seasonable this weekend, but with more \nclouds on Sunday.\n\nForecast outlook for this weekend now looks than it was looking 24 \nhours ago. The last couple cycles of deterministic and ensemble \nguidance now conveys the idea that the strong high pressure which \nbuilds in on Saturday remains strong enough through Sunday to \nsuppress rain showers lurching northward from the southern waters \naway from land. Sunday still features a considerable amount of cloud \ncover but dry weather looks to prevail, even as a pretty potent 500 \nmb shortwave in the northern stream treks eastward along the \nUS/Canada border Sunday night, draping a dry cold front southward \ninto the Sunday night/early Monday timeframe. \n\nSaturday looks to be the pick of the weekend with mostly sunny skies \nand highs in the mid to upper 60s, but lower 60s along the \neastern/southeast coast due to NEly onshore breezes. Highs Sunday \nshould be a couple degrees cooler with more cloud cover.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 4...Much cooler autumn airmass settles over \nSouthern New England early to middle of next week.\n\nModel guidance remains consistent on a dry, blustery and a much \ncooler autumn airmass for early to mid next week. This occurs in \nresponse to a strong upper trough establishing itself over the \nNortheast, with a sfc 1030 mb anticyclone over the Gt Lakes settling \nover New England by Tue night. Strong cold advection begins Monday \nafternoon with 925 mb temps dropping to as low as -2C by 12z Tue! \n\nExpect full sunshine and dry weather, but rather breezy on Monday \nwith a tight northwesterly pressure gradient, with highs in the low \nto mid 60s and lows in the upper 30s to middle 40s. Even cooler \nweather arrives by Tue, with highs only in the mid 50s. Depending on \nhow quickly winds slacken Tue night, some areas could have their \nfirst frost since last Spring, with lows in the low to mid 30s \ninterior, upper 30s/mid 40s for the cities and low to mid 40s near \nthe eastern/southeast coast.",
